    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nThis essay examines the widespread practice of prenatal screening for Down syndrome and its startling statistical link to high rates of abortion to highlight the implicit bias against disability that is common in clinical presentations of prenatal care. Drawing on moving first-hand narrative accounts from parents of children with Down’s, her research reveals the flawed rhetoric doctors employ when informing parents of positive results, which are counter to the medical establishment’s Hippocratic oath to “first, do no harm.”",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
